"You’ve probably heard this a thousand times, that the world is changing so fast.\n\nSome skills are slowly heading the direction of the dinosaur. Some are ageless regardless of what is thrown at them. And some are becoming almost impossible to build a career without.\n\nSo, in the middle of all this, I’ve been thinking about the kind of career I want to build.\n\nNot job titles, no. Ability!\n\nYou see, the more I look around, the less I believe one job title can explain a career. People have jobs, take on projects, sell stuff, build businesses, and can combine all of these things just with the skills they have.\n\nSo I’m building my own list. Of what I’m betting on for myself.\n\nNot everything I want to learn, nah! That could require a whole book. Just the skills I want to be disgustingly good at. Building them, day by day.\n\n1. Research\nTop three skills I want to be great at, and it ain’t number three. I’ve worked across marketing, design, and product management, and research is the part that always gets me ticking.\n\nThe barrier to creating almost anything today is on the floor. But research is that thing that lets you figure out if they should even exist.\n\nDiscovering that people respond better to one style of writing than another is research. To learn why a competitor thrived in one area but failing in another is research.\n\nAnd my favourite part is when the evidence challenges my own hypothesis. Few things give me instant dopamine as finding a pattern I hadn’t noticed before.\n\nI want more of those.\n\n2. Sales\nOof!\n\nEven writing the word makes me slightly nervous. That’s probably a sign I need to get better at it.\n\nSales, at its simplest, is the ability to exchange value for revenue.\n\nThe founder convincing an investor. The applicant convincing a company. The freelancer explaining why her work costs ₦500,000 instead of ₦50,000. The friend convincing everyone that this restaurant is where you should eat tonight.\n\nAll of it is sales. And that’s why I think of this as an art, rather than math. Of course, there are pipelines, conversion rates, margins, and all of those responsible adult things.\n\nBut this is psychology.\n\nIt is understanding how people think, what they value, what they fear, what they desire, and what finally moves them from maybe to yes. And I covet that ability. Even if for now, I can only touch the hem of its garment.\n\n3. Writing\nPeople say nobody reads anymore, so why do you write? Which is strange because half the internet appears to be people arguing over something somebody wrote.\n\nA person starts a company because they read about the possibilities. Someone makes sense of a confusing career because a stranger on Reddit explained it in clear terms. You bought that course because one sentence made you think, “Wait, I need this.” Writing keeps sneaking into everything.\n\nIt’s the one thing I’ve been doing, passion or income, for over five years and somehow still feel far from where I want to be.\n\nSure, AI’s there to turn our thoughts into text, but it’s also why I’ve found writing more interesting. Because when you open a platform to find LLMs talking to each other, there’s something satisfying about finding writing that feels like someone indeed thought it through.\n\nI’m a sucker for it, and it’s why I gave this its own number.\n\nNothing clarifies my own thinking quite like writing does, and I want to be excellent at it. At translating what is in my head across essays, copy, strategy, reports, storytelling, and whatever the work demands.\n\n4. AI orchestration\nNow to the obvious one.\n\nYou almost cannot discuss careers in 2026 without AI entering the room to pull up a chair.\n\nDay by day, I see the things it quietly steals from us, a whole lot. But I’m more interested in its innate capabilities.\n\nHow do I break a difficult problem into pieces? Which parts should a machine handle? How do I verify what comes back? How do I connect tools so that ten manual steps become two? How do I ensure those steps skipped aren’t breaking the natural order of things?\n\nThe ethics in AI and the power it offers, far beyond the everyday slop, is where I want to invest in. The times demand it. And it’s worth exploring.\n\n5. Negotiation\nIf you work with humans, congratulations.\n\nYou will disagree.\n\nSomeone wants Friday, you want Monday. Someone thinks the project needs twelve features, you’re fighting for four. Someone offers ₦200,000, your spirit says ₦500,000.\n\nWelcome to Negotiation!\n\nI’d be honest, it can be hard. I mean, how can we arrive somewhere I can live with without destroying the relationship on our way there? And that requires understanding incentives. Knowing what you can concede, knowing what you cannot. Asking better questions, and occasionally learning that age-old professional skill of keeping quiet.\n\nCareers are full of moments where nobody simply hands you what you deserve because you have been such a lovely person. Salary, scope, deadlines, credit, ownership, opportunities. You will have to ask. And I must master how.\n\n6. Distribution\nWhen I think about distribution, I think about visibility in all the right places.\n\nWho needs this right now? Where are they? Who can help it travel? How does this reach them? How do I earn the right to appear there? How do I build enough trust that people are willing to pass it along?\n\nAnd sometimes, that could mean building online. Sometimes partnerships, communities, a newsletter, a WhatsApp group, an event, a creator, or one person mentioning your name in a room where your own voice cannot reach.\n\nI want to understand how those systems are built, because great work that is hidden is still, unfortunately, hidden.\n\n7. Financial literacy\nOkay, we probably don’t talk about this enough.\n\nBut you know, you can be building an impressive career and still be terrible at managing what that career earns you. As I spend time around my environment, I’m starting to see that making money and knowing what to do with money are two different abilities.\n\nYou can increase your income and increase your lifestyle at exactly the same speed, and then realise you aren’t actually moving as you’d hoped. And even more common? The money comes in, but the month is longer than that account balance. So financial literacy is understanding what happens after I earn. How much is kept? How much is spent? How much is invested? What risks are taken? What compounds?\n\nY’know, I’m almost certain the big names we know today had to master this skill sometime in their career. I want to understand money well enough that earning more isn’t the only strategy available to me.\n\nAnd not just for myself, but in the work we do. How do taxes, inflation, debt, assets, cash flow, and opportunity cost shape the decisions we make? Because income matters, but knowing what to do with income matters just as much.\n\n8. Communication\nThis is the biggest one for me.\n\nI saved it for last because it may sit underneath almost everything else.\n\nGood lord, communication is powerful.\n\nWritten, spoken, visual! It can land opportunities or destroy them. It can align teams or be the reason for chaos. Because people who communicate exceptionally well can change how others see a situation. They can create urgency. They can make complexity feel simple. Or just make people care.\n\nI was rewatching Attack on Titan recently, and Captain Erwin’s speech before his soldiers rode toward “certain death” gave me goosebumps all over again. To create such action with just words. So I think of excellent communicators as not just leaders, but demi-gods.\n\nNow ideally, none of us will need to persuade our coworkers to charge directly at the Beast Titan. But you get the drift. Leadership requires communication. To be able to help people understand what matters, why it matters, and what needs to happen next.\n\nAnd that is a skill I want to spend a lifetime improving. Dramatic? Maybe!\n\nBut what’s the bigger bet in all of this? Research. Sales. Writing. AI orchestration. Negotiation. Distribution. Financial literacy. Communication. They may look like eight different skills but I see them as something I’d like to call…\n\nagency!\n\nThe ability to understand what is happening, decide what’s worth doing, create something useful, explain it clearly, get it in front of people, persuade them, negotiate what happens next, manage the value that comes back, and ensure that this 6-minute piece can even be more condensed.\n\nThat’s my bet for thriving in this ever-changing time.\n\nNow, I’m curious. If you were choosing the skills to become great at, for the next phase of your career, what would be on the list?"
